How Sleepwalking Works — asset sources & licences

Picture   All 3D models (house, people, brain, graph, tiles, props) are low-poly primitives built in code with three.js
          (world.js). No textures, no generated or stock images. Post-processing adapted from the Lemo-Opuscar library's
          Low-poly Isometric demo (post.js; github.com/lemomo-ai/lemo-opuscar, MIT). three.js, MIT.
Music     Original, event-driven score written in code (music/score.py).
          Samples (CC0): Versilian Community Sample Library (Versilian Studios).
Sound FX  Synthesized in code (mix.py + the library's core/audio/sfx.py). No recorded samples.
Voice     Kokoro-82M TTS (kokoro-onnx), Apache-2.0, voice af_heart.
Fonts     Josefin Sans (Santiago Orozco), Quicksand (Andrew Paglinawan), SIL Open Font License 1.1; files + licences in fonts/.

Facts and sources
- Sleepwalking is a partial arousal from NREM sleep, typically in the first third of the night; sleepwalkers "can be led
  back to bed and do not need to be awakened" — Stallman, Aust Fam Physician 2017 (racgp.org.au/afp/2017/august).
- During an episode from slow-wave sleep (N3), the motor cortex and cingulate showed wake-like activity while frontal and
  parietal association cortices kept deep-sleep delta waves — Terzaghi et al., Sleep 2009 (PMC2647795).
- 12-month prevalence 5.0% of children, 1.5% of adults (meta-analysis of 51 studies) — Stallman & Kohler, PLoS ONE 2016 (PMC5104520).
- Recall ranges from none to complete; runs in families (twin studies); triggers include sleep loss, stress, fever and some
  medications — reviews above.
- The night graph is an illustrative typical night (deep sleep concentrated early), not one person's recording.
